# Cursor Rules

This project is a Go Fiber backend with a React/TypeScript/Tailwind frontend. The built frontend is **embedded into a single Go binary** for deployment.

## Stack

- **Backend:** Go 1.23+, Go Fiber v2 — serves embedded static files and API.
- **Frontend:** React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS v4, Vite — build output goes to `dist/` at repo root.
- **Deploy:** Single-stage Docker (Alpine), default port 80.

## Project layout

- `main.go` — Fiber app: `//go:embed dist/*`, API under `/api/*`, static + SPA fallback at `/`.
- `frontend/` — Vite app; `vite.config.ts` sets `outDir: "../dist"`. Dev server proxies `/api` to backend.
- `dist/` — Generated by `npm run build` in `frontend/`; do not edit by hand. Go embeds this.
- `dibbla-task.yaml` — Dibbla task definition (env, tools, build steps, dev servers).
- `Dockerfile` — Multi-stage: build frontend → build Go (with dist) → minimal Alpine image.
- `APP.md` — App store page: short description of what the app does. Must exist and be kept up-to-date when the app changes.

## Conventions

- **Go:** Uses `godotenv` for optional `.env`. `ENV_HELLO_NAME` is required; `PORT` defaults to 80 (8080 in dev).
- **API:** REST under `/api/` (e.g. `GET /api/hello`). Add new routes in `main.go` before the static/filesystem middleware.
- **Frontend:** Use Tailwind v4 with `@theme` in `frontend/src/index.css`. Prefer design tokens: `dibbla-green`, `dibbla-dark`, `dibbla-card`.
- **Static/SPA:** Keep the existing order: serve filesystem at `/`, then catch-all to `index.html` for client-side routing.
- **APP.md:** Ensure `APP.md` exists in the repo and is kept up-to-date with the latest description of what the app does. It is used as the app store page for the app; update it whenever features or purpose change.

## Workflow

- **Local run:** `cd frontend && npm install && npm run build && cd ..` then `go run main.go` (or use Dibbla task).
- **Dev:** Backend on 8080, Vite dev server on 5173 with API proxy to backend.
- **Docker:** `docker build -t getting-started-1 .` — ensure `dist/` exists (frontend built) before building image or Go binary.

When changing the frontend, rebuild (`npm run build` in `frontend/`) before running or building the Go app so `dist/` is up to date.

## Databases

If the user asks to add a database:

- **ORM:** Use GORM. Add the dependency and open the DB in `main.go` (or a small `db` package) using a connection string from the environment.
- **Connection string:** Do not hardcode. Read from an env var (e.g. `DATABASE_URL` or `DB_CONNECTION`). To get the value from Dibbla, use the **Dibbla CLI skill** and run e.g. `dibbla secrets get DB_CONNECTION` if a secret with that name exists.
- **Create and manage databases via Dibbla only:** Do not add custom SQL or Postgres provisioning code. Use the Dibbla CLI so the app’s database gets the right setup (including pgvector). First check existing databases to avoid name conflicts: `dibbla db list`. Create a new database with `dibbla db create <db_name>`. See `.claude/skills/dibbla-cli-skill/SKILL.md` and `dibbla --skill-prompt` for full docs.
- **Local dev:** Use the same env var in `.env` (e.g. a local Postgres URL). Document the variable in `.env.example` if needed.

## Deployment

When the user asks about deployment, use the **Dibbla CLI skill** (see `.claude/skills/dibbla-cli-skill/SKILL.md`):

- Deploy with `dibbla deploy .` (no `--yes` needed). Use `--force` to overwrite an existing deployment with the same alias.
- Pass env vars from `.env` using `--env VAR=val` for each variable the app needs (including database URLs if stored in secrets).
- If the app listens on a port other than 80, set it with `--port <port>` (match the port in the Dockerfile).
- For updates to an existing deployment, use `dibbla update`; run `dibbla --skill-prompt` for full options.
